NAME
    chmod - change file permission bits

SYNOPSIS
    chmod MODE FILE [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
    Sets each FILE's permission bits to MODE. MODE is a 1-4 digit
    octal integer (e.g. 755, 644, 0600). Symbolic modes like u+x or
    go-w are NOT supported — use the octal form.

    The low 9 bits map as the usual rwx-rwx-rwx triple: owner, group,
    other. The kernel SYS_CHMOD enforces that only the file's owner
    or root may change permissions.

EXAMPLES
    chmod 755 script.sh         # rwxr-xr-x
    chmod 600 /etc/secret       # rw-------
    chmod 0644 /tmp/log.txt     # leading 0 allowed but optional

EXIT STATUS
    0   every FILE was chmod'd
    1   at least one chmod failed (missing, not owner, bad MODE)

SEE ALSO
    ls(1), id(1)

NOTES
    No symbolic mode (chmod u+x). The octal form covers every real
    use case in <80 chars of code per script. Added in session 83.
